<header class="ed-hero bg-inverse pb-14 pt-32 text-inverse-ink md:pt-40"><div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6"><p class="mb-4 text-sm font-bold uppercase tracking-widest text-accent">The Naked Tech field guide</p><h1 class="text-4xl font-extrabold tracking-tight md:text-5xl">How to look for a missing email without deleting anything</h1><p class="ed-deck">Search first, check the account and folders, then ask the sender about delivery. Do not delete or change unfamiliar settings as a first response.</p></div></header>
<article class="ed-guide bg-canvas py-12 md:py-16" aria-label="How to look for a missing email without deleting anything"><div class="ed-layout">
{{ contents([{"id": "start", "title": "Find your starting point"}, {"id": "correct-account", "title": "Confirm the account before searching"}, {"id": "search-folders-filters", "title": "Search broadly without changing anything"}, {"id": "delivery-and-forwarding", "title": "Check the delivery story"}, {"id": "related", "title": "What to read next"}]) }}
<div class="ed-reading guide-reading text-lg">
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">A missing email may be in another folder, hidden by a display filter, in a different account, or never accepted by the provider. Work through the mailbox you can access and record what you checked. This guide helps you look without deleting messages or promising that a message can be restored.</p>
<section class="ed-start" id="start" aria-labelledby="start-heading"><p class="ed-label">Start here</p><h2 id="start-heading">Search in a controlled order</h2><p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Confirm the mailbox first, then search broadly, inspect delivery settings, and ask the sender for evidence if needed.</p><div class="ed-choices">
{{ choice("Confirm the mailbox", "Check the full address or account name before searching. Similar accounts can have separate mailboxes.", "#correct-account", "startup", "/img/guides/email-troubleshooting-v1.webp", "A laptop displaying a conceptual email inbox.", "Read this part") }}
{{ choice("Search folders", "Look across the relevant folders and clear display filters before narrowing the search.", "#search-folders-filters", "applications", "/img/guides/email-troubleshooting-v1.webp", "Mail folders and an envelope beside a magnifying glass.", "Read this part") }}
{{ choice("Ask about delivery", "Ask the sender for the sending time, address and any bounce or error message they received.", "#delivery-and-forwarding", "online", "/img/guides/email-troubleshooting-v1.webp", "Two envelopes with arrows pointing in opposite directions.", "Read this part") }}
</div></section>
<h2 class="ed-section-heading" id="correct-account"><span aria-hidden="true">01</span>Confirm the account before searching</h2>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Check the complete address shown in webmail or the app. If you have personal, older or recovery accounts, a sender may have used a different address than expected. Search the provider's webmail where possible, because a local app may only show mail it has already downloaded.</p><p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Check the account's storage notice as well. If the provider cannot accept new messages because its storage is full, the message may have bounced before it became part of the mailbox. A full recipient mailbox is different from a message being hidden in a folder.</p><p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Some mail is stored only on a device or in a local mail archive. That mail may not appear in webmail. Avoid changing account settings until you know where the older messages are stored.</p>
<h2 class="ed-section-heading" id="search-folders-filters"><span aria-hidden="true">02</span>Search broadly without changing anything</h2>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">In Gmail on a computer, open Show search options beside the search bar, then choose “Mail &amp; Spam &amp; Trash” in the Search dropdown when looking for a message. Try a sender address, a distinctive subject fragment or a short phrase. For example, enter <code>person@example.com</code> in From and <code>invoice</code> in Subject. These are example search details; use the sender and subject you actually expect. Leave an option blank when you do not know it, rather than guessing.</p><p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Do not assume that All Mail includes Spam or Trash. Check the relevant folders separately, including Archive, Junk or Spam, and Trash. Also clear date, sender, category or unread-only display filters that could be hiding a result.</p><p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Google notes that filters can skip the Inbox or delete incoming messages. Review filters, forwarding and related settings without editing unfamiliar security options. See <a class="font-semibold text-accent-ink underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-ink" href="https://support.google.com/mail/answer/7015314?hl=en">Google's missing Gmail messages guidance</a>.</p>
<h2 class="ed-section-heading" id="delivery-and-forwarding"><span aria-hidden="true">03</span>Check the delivery story</h2>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Ask the sender to confirm the exact address, approximate sending time and whether they received a bounce. A bounce may point to a wrong address, a full mailbox, a server problem or filtering. It is evidence about that sending attempt, not proof that every message to the account is affected.</p><p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">If you find forwarding that you do not recognise, stop making changes and use <a class="font-semibold text-accent-ink underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-ink" href="/guides/check-account-access-after-a-scam/">check account access after a scam</a> for the security response. Do not disable security controls or delete unfamiliar rules simply to make the Inbox look normal.</p><p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Searching cannot guarantee recovery. A message that was never accepted, was permanently deleted, or exists only in a local archive may not be available in the online mailbox.</p>{% call note("care", "Take care", "Preserve what you find") %}<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Finding a message in a folder is different from restoring it. Do not empty Trash, remove rules or rebuild an account while investigating. Preserve what you find and note the account, folder, search terms and time checked.</p>{% endcall %}
